#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f32536-8553-5777-bd2f-2bc5fde22ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51f32536-8553-5777-bd2f-2bc5fde22ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092} :answer-field/id #uuid "de40c871-decf-5ba8-847c-45fa59833e09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca2c3569-8e4d-568d-a6c0-54b175067ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca2c3569-8e4d-568d-a6c0-54b175067ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345093} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49a6956-c656-5955-945d-f6b90c3bdc24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b910099-c567-5648-8f68-a3d99b4db76a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b910099-c567-5648-8f68-a3d99b4db76a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345094} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff376e21-19b8-5167-a4e9-05414471e1f1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dedb72cf-47cf-5e98-a478-6359d9544476" :question/math-academy-id "q-181610" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063256/assets/fbd778f48199acd5b731e739cb527a990ba28a8d88981d50554d135e853f8815.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c088c2b-a005-57fb-ba1f-103fa4afad25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097} {:answer/id #uuid "60512c4c-8ffa-5213-9bfa-767c50d061a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} 